1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:07,494
Before Google became a verb, before
Facebook connected billions, and before

2
00:00:07,534 --> 00:00:11,604
smartphones put the internet in
everyone's pocket, there was Yahoo.

3
00:00:12,133 --> 00:00:15,420
For millions of people,
Yahoo was the internet.

4
00:00:16,048 --> 00:00:20,065
It was where they searched, checked their
email, followed the news, and discovered

5
00:00:20,105 --> 00:00:21,575
what was happening online.

6
00:00:22,319 --> 00:00:26,275
At the height of the dot-com boom, the
company was valued at more than one hundred

7
00:00:26,315 --> 00:00:27,585
billion dollars.

8
00:00:28,562 --> 00:00:33,766
But in 2017, Yahoo's core operating
business was sold to Verizon.

9
00:00:34,392 --> 00:00:37,989
How did an internet pioneer
lose control of its future?

10
00:00:39,803 --> 00:00:42,073
June thirteenth, 2017.

11
00:00:42,618 --> 00:00:47,042
Verizon completed its acquisition
of Yahoo's operating business.

12
00:00:47,654 --> 00:00:50,836
The amended purchase agreement had set the
price at approximately four point four

13
00:00:50,876 --> 00:00:54,906
eight billion dollars,
subject to adjustments.

14
00:00:55,603 --> 00:00:59,057
The final accounting reflected a
different closing consideration.

15
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:05,422
And importantly, Yahoo's valuable investments
in Alibaba and Yahoo Japan were not

16
00:01:05,462 --> 00:01:06,462
part of the sale.

17
00:01:06,887 --> 00:01:10,010
So this was not the entire
company's value disappearing.

18
00:01:10,586 --> 00:01:14,869
It was the end of Yahoo as an
independent internet operating business.

19
00:01:15,386 --> 00:01:19,115
To understand how it happened, we
need to return to the beginning.

20
00:01:20,789 --> 00:01:28,102
In 1994, two Stanford graduate students,
Jerry Yang and David Filo, began organizing

21
00:01:28,142 --> 00:01:30,072
websites into a directory.

22
00:01:30,601 --> 00:01:34,826
They called it Jerry and David's
Guide to the World Wide Web.

23
00:01:35,353 --> 00:01:36,658
The idea was simple.

24
00:01:37,149 --> 00:01:40,696
The internet was growing faster
than people could navigate it.

25
00:01:41,072 --> 00:01:43,387
Someone needed to organize the chaos.

26
00:01:43,982 --> 00:01:46,173
Their project became Yahoo.

27
00:01:46,817 --> 00:01:52,298
And as the web expanded, Yahoo became one
of its most recognizable destinations.

28
00:01:53,155 --> 00:01:56,118
The company went public in April 1996.

29
00:01:57,054 --> 00:01:59,859
It had found something
extraordinarily valuable.

30
00:02:00,370 --> 00:02:03,573
A place at the beginning of
the internet user's journey.

31
00:02:05,263 --> 00:02:08,613
Yahoo expanded far beyond
its original directory.

32
00:02:09,253 --> 00:02:13,880
Email. News. Finance. Sports.
Shopping. Entertainment.

33
00:02:14,384 --> 00:02:18,603
Its strategy was to become a central
destination for everything people wanted

34
00:02:18,643 --> 00:02:19,643
online.

35
00:02:20,048 --> 00:02:23,336
That created enormous
opportunities for advertising.

36
00:02:23,930 --> 00:02:29,063
The more users visited Yahoo's services,
the more valuable its audience became to

37
00:02:29,103 --> 00:02:30,103
advertisers.

38
00:02:30,541 --> 00:02:32,897
For a time, the strategy worked.

39
00:02:33,359 --> 00:02:35,560
Yahoo wasn't simply a search engine.

40
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:40,546
It was a collection of internet services,
held together by one of the world's most

41
00:02:40,586 --> 00:02:41,996
recognizable brands.

42
00:02:42,546 --> 00:02:45,370
But a different model of
the internet was emerging.

43
00:02:47,081 --> 00:02:49,452
Google approached the
internet differently.

44
00:02:49,964 --> 00:02:55,224
Instead of asking users to browse through
a directory or spend time on a portal, it

45
00:02:55,264 --> 00:02:58,754
concentrated on helping them
find the most relevant answer.

46
00:02:59,182 --> 00:03:02,241
Its search technology
improved as the web grew.

47
00:03:02,723 --> 00:03:07,921
And its advertising business increasingly
connected commercial intent with search

48
00:03:07,961 --> 00:03:08,961
results.

49
00:03:09,312 --> 00:03:13,574
Yahoo had search technology and
search partnerships of its own.

50
00:03:13,968 --> 00:03:18,052
But Google's focus created a
powerful competitive advantage.

51
00:03:18,631 --> 00:03:23,395
The contest was no longer simply about
attracting the largest audience.

52
00:03:23,772 --> 00:03:27,616
It was about owning the moment
when someone wanted an answer.

53
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:34,209
One of Yahoo's most famous missed
opportunities involves Google itself.

54
00:03:34,822 --> 00:03:39,973
Contemporary reporting describes acquisition
discussions in the early two-thousands.

55
00:03:40,286 --> 00:03:44,719
In one widely reported account, Yahoo
explored buying Google for roughly three

56
00:03:44,759 --> 00:03:48,169
billion dollars, but a
deal never happened.

57
00:03:48,595 --> 00:03:52,512
The negotiations were more complicated
than the familiar story that Yahoo simply

58
00:03:52,552 --> 00:03:55,022
rejected Google for one million dollars.

59
00:03:55,978 --> 00:03:58,337
What matters is the strategic question.

60
00:03:58,928 --> 00:04:03,938
Yahoo had an opportunity to become more
deeply involved in Google's future.

61
00:04:04,337 --> 00:04:08,729
Instead, Google developed into its
most formidable search competitor.

62
00:04:10,542 --> 00:04:12,684
Yahoo did not stand still.

63
00:04:13,025 --> 00:04:18,996
It acquired search technology company
Inktomi and paid-search specialist Overture.

64
00:04:19,309 --> 00:04:24,935
These acquisitions gave Yahoo important
capabilities in web search and advertising.

65
00:04:25,515 --> 00:04:31,216
In 2004, Yahoo stopped relying on Google's
search results and launched its own

66
00:04:31,256 --> 00:04:32,506
search technology.

67
00:04:33,019 --> 00:04:37,826
The company was investing in the very
market where Google was gaining strength.

68
00:04:38,277 --> 00:04:42,461
But owning the pieces of a search business
was not the same as executing a superior

69
00:04:42,501 --> 00:04:43,871
search strategy.

70
00:04:44,285 --> 00:04:46,431
Google continued building momentum.

71
00:04:47,074 --> 00:04:51,077
Yahoo was now fighting to reclaim
ground it had once helped define.

72
00:04:52,898 --> 00:04:55,862
Yahoo's strength had become
a strategic challenge.

73
00:04:56,412 --> 00:05:02,388
It was a portal, a media company, an email
provider, an advertising platform, and a

74
00:05:02,428 --> 00:05:03,428
search competitor.

75
00:05:03,908 --> 00:05:05,957
Each business required investment.

76
00:05:06,818 --> 00:05:08,390
Each faced different rivals.

77
00:05:09,030 --> 00:05:11,473
And each competed for
management attention.

78
00:05:12,087 --> 00:05:14,763
Diversification can protect a company.

79
00:05:15,190 --> 00:05:19,920
But it can also make it difficult to decide
where the next great advantage will come

80
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:20,839
from.

81
00:05:20,879 --> 00:05:24,767
Google had a clear center of
gravity in search and advertising.

82
00:05:25,301 --> 00:05:28,685
Yahoo had an extraordinary
collection of assets.

83
00:05:29,303 --> 00:05:33,034
The harder question was how those
assets should fit together.

84
00:05:34,728 --> 00:05:39,257
By 2006, another internet
revolution was taking shape.

85
00:05:39,692 --> 00:05:44,412
Social networking was transforming how
people discovered information and interacted

86
00:05:44,452 --> 00:05:45,452
online.

87
00:05:45,754 --> 00:05:48,729
Yahoo held acquisition
discussions with Facebook.

88
00:05:49,263 --> 00:05:54,084
Reports describe negotiations around a
billion-dollar valuation, with disagreements

89
00:05:54,124 --> 00:05:57,254
over price and the company's future.

90
00:05:57,587 --> 00:05:59,041
No deal was completed.

91
00:05:59,619 --> 00:06:04,535
It is tempting to calculate what Facebook
would eventually be worth and call that

92
00:06:04,575 --> 00:06:06,365
Yahoo's loss.

93
00:06:06,431 --> 00:06:10,374
But that assumes the acquisition would
have happened, succeeded, and preserved

94
00:06:10,414 --> 00:06:11,724
Facebook's growth.

95
00:06:12,248 --> 00:06:17,155
The more useful question is why Yahoo
struggled to establish a comparable position

96
00:06:17,195 --> 00:06:18,705
in social networking.

97
00:06:20,425 --> 00:06:22,654
Then came an extraordinary offer.

98
00:06:23,121 --> 00:06:27,620
On February first, 2008, Microsoft
proposed acquiring Yahoo for approximately

99
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:32,670
forty-four point six billion
dollars in equity value.

100
00:06:33,054 --> 00:06:37,947
Microsoft wanted to strengthen its
position in search and online advertising.

101
00:06:38,567 --> 00:06:40,858
The negotiations continued for months.

102
00:06:41,467 --> 00:06:46,576
Microsoft later said it had increased its
proposal by roughly five billion dollars.

103
00:06:47,556 --> 00:06:49,865
But the companies could
not agree on terms.

104
00:06:50,434 --> 00:06:53,076
In May, Microsoft withdrew its bid.

105
00:06:53,541 --> 00:06:55,535
Yahoo remained independent.

106
00:06:56,102 --> 00:07:00,402
It also remained under pressure to prove
that independence could create greater

107
00:07:00,442 --> 00:07:01,442
value.

108
00:07:02,935 --> 00:07:07,393
A year later, Yahoo and Microsoft
announced a search partnership.

109
00:07:07,861 --> 00:07:12,959
Under the agreement, Microsoft's search
technology would power Yahoo's algorithmic

110
00:07:12,999 --> 00:07:18,639
search results, while Yahoo retained
important responsibilities for its own user

111
00:07:18,679 --> 00:07:21,569
experience and advertising relationships.

112
00:07:22,213 --> 00:07:25,640
The deal was designed to
create scale against Google.

113
00:07:26,152 --> 00:07:28,915
But it also marked a
major strategic change.

114
00:07:29,493 --> 00:07:34,617
Yahoo, once a pioneer of online discovery,
was increasingly relying on another

115
00:07:34,657 --> 00:07:37,327
company's underlying search technology.

116
00:07:37,794 --> 00:07:40,323
It could still serve millions of users.

117
00:07:40,905 --> 00:07:44,621
The question was whether it could
control the technology that shaped their

118
00:07:44,661 --> 00:07:45,661
experience.

119
00:07:47,315 --> 00:07:51,556
While Yahoo was fighting over search,
the internet was moving again.

120
00:07:51,961 --> 00:07:55,187
Smartphones changed how
people accessed information.

121
00:07:55,831 --> 00:08:00,517
Applications reduced the need to begin
every online journey at a traditional web

122
00:08:00,557 --> 00:08:01,541
portal.

123
00:08:01,581 --> 00:08:06,022
Social networks changed how content
was discovered and distributed.

124
00:08:06,062 --> 00:08:10,145
And digital advertising became
increasingly sophisticated.

125
00:08:10,725 --> 00:08:17,067
Yahoo still had popular services,
including Mail, Finance, News, and Sports.

126
00:08:17,686 --> 00:08:20,738
But audience scale alone
was no longer enough.

127
00:08:21,323 --> 00:08:26,025
The company needed stronger positions in
mobile products, advertising technology,

128
00:08:26,435 --> 00:08:30,265
and the platforms shaping the next
generation of internet behavior.

129
00:08:32,037 --> 00:08:38,393
In July 2012, Yahoo appointed former
Google executive Marissa Mayer as chief

130
00:08:38,433 --> 00:08:39,433
executive.

131
00:08:39,784 --> 00:08:43,011
Her arrival created
expectations of a turnaround.

132
00:08:43,730 --> 00:08:50,096
Yahoo invested in mobile applications,
product redesigns, talent, and acquisitions.

133
00:08:50,898 --> 00:08:56,482
The company acquired Tumblr in 2013 for
approximately one point one billion dollars.

134
00:08:57,481 --> 00:09:02,078
But a large acquisition and renewed
product investment could not automatically

135
00:09:02,118 --> 00:09:04,528
restore Yahoo's competitive position.

136
00:09:05,164 --> 00:09:09,587
The company was trying to reinvent itself
while facing entrenched competitors in

137
00:09:09,627 --> 00:09:12,917
search, social media,
and digital advertising.

138
00:09:13,428 --> 00:09:16,417
Time was becoming one of
its scarcest resources.

139
00:09:18,318 --> 00:09:22,146
In 2016, Verizon agreed to buy Yahoo's
operating business for approximately four

140
00:09:22,186 --> 00:09:25,136
point eight three billion dollars.

141
00:09:26,035 --> 00:09:29,801
Then Yahoo disclosed major
historical data breaches.

142
00:09:30,421 --> 00:09:35,147
The revelations complicated the transaction
and raised serious questions about

143
00:09:35,187 --> 00:09:36,977
security and accountability.

144
00:09:37,675 --> 00:09:42,709
In February 2017, the companies agreed to
reduce the stated purchase price by three

145
00:09:42,749 --> 00:09:46,139
hundred and fifty million dollars.

146
00:09:46,995 --> 00:09:49,287
The transaction closed that June.

147
00:09:49,831 --> 00:09:52,714
Yahoo's operating business
became part of Verizon.

148
00:09:53,324 --> 00:09:59,491
Its remaining investment holdings were
separated into a company called Altaba.

149
00:10:00,094 --> 00:10:04,043
The original internet empire had
been fundamentally transformed.

150
00:10:06,004 --> 00:10:09,425
So what killed Yahoo's position
as an internet leader?

151
00:10:09,905 --> 00:10:11,697
Not one missed acquisition.

152
00:10:12,253 --> 00:10:14,103
And not one chief executive.

153
00:10:14,670 --> 00:10:16,932
The evidence points to a broader pattern.

154
00:10:17,411 --> 00:10:19,558
An uncertain strategic identity.

155
00:10:20,066 --> 00:10:24,841
Difficulty translating enormous audience
reach into defensible technology

156
00:10:24,881 --> 00:10:25,991
leadership.

157
00:10:26,544 --> 00:10:30,107
Intensifying competition
in search and advertising.

158
00:10:30,711 --> 00:10:35,224
And a series of acquisitions and partnerships
that did not establish a lasting

159
00:10:35,264 --> 00:10:38,694
advantage in the next generation
of internet platforms.

160
00:10:39,298 --> 00:10:42,063
Yahoo was successful at attracting users.

161
00:10:42,596 --> 00:10:47,363
But attracting users and controlling the
future of the internet were becoming very

162
00:10:47,403 --> 00:10:48,453
different things.

163
00:10:50,327 --> 00:10:54,837
Yahoo's story is not simply about a
company that missed Google or Facebook.

164
00:10:55,416 --> 00:11:00,026
It is about the difference between being
where the internet is today and building

165
00:11:00,066 --> 00:11:01,956
what the internet will need tomorrow.

166
00:11:02,486 --> 00:11:05,546
Yahoo helped millions of
people discover the web.

167
00:11:06,015 --> 00:11:10,386
It created services that became
part of everyday digital life.

168
00:11:10,891 --> 00:11:16,185
But the industry moved toward new
technologies, new platforms, and new business

169
00:11:16,225 --> 00:11:17,225
models.

170
00:11:17,448 --> 00:11:19,737
Yahoo struggled to lead that transition.

171
00:11:20,230 --> 00:11:21,717
And that is the verdict.

172
00:11:22,284 --> 00:11:26,953
Being the front door to an industry does
not mean you will own what comes next.

173
00:11:27,436 --> 00:11:29,242
Every brand leaves evidence.

